.he "packsrc (1)                  9/28/26                  packsrc (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
packsrc
- pack a tree into tar files, or a manifest into disks
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
packsrc tar tree outdir budget
.br
packsrc part manifest outdir blocks inodes
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Packsrc sorts a set of files into volumes under a size budget.  It has
two modes, because a distribution has two kinds of payload: what
compresses, which is cheaper to carry as one tar than as a thousand
files, and what does not, which is written out unpacked.
.sp
The unit of packing is one directory's own files, and it is the unit
both modes use.  A unit goes on one volume whole.  A directory that
will not fit on a volume even when the volume is empty is the only
thing that is ever split.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
TAR
.in +5
.br
Packsrc tar walks tree and writes a series of tar files into outdir,
each one as close to budget 512 byte blocks as the tree allows, with
the members of each written to list.N and the archive itself to
src.tar.N, numbered from 1.
.sp
Units are taken in sorted order and accumulated into one tar until it
would go over budget, at which point the last unit is backed off and
starts the next tar.  One pass, and no search: a candidate's size is
computed from the file sizes rather than found by building it.
.sp
A unit that is over budget on its own goes into a tar by itself rather
than being cut in half, and the tar it lands in is reported as over.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
PART
.in +5
.br
Packsrc part reads manifest, a list of size path lines with the path
relative to the image's root, and writes list.N into outdir for each
volume, one path to a line.  The output is a partition: every path of
the manifest appears exactly once, on one disk.
.sp
blocks is the volume's free blocks, two below the filesystem size less
the inodes; the boot area is in front of the filesystem and is not
counted either.  A file costs what bmap charges, which is not its
length in blocks: eight addresses in the inode are direct, the ninth
is one indirect block, and past 1792 blocks a second level is added one
block per 256.
.sp
inodes limits the volume too, and it is not a formality.  A directory
costs an inode and costs blocks, 32 entries to a block at 16 bytes an
entry, so 224 names in one directory is seven blocks that hold no file
data.  Every directory above a file has to exist for the file to land,
and a directory's parent grows by a block each time the parent's own
entry count crosses a multiple of 32, which a disk holding one file
from /usr/src/cmd pays for, having to build /usr, /usr/src and
/usr/src/cmd to hold it.
.sp
A unit that will not fit on an empty disk goes a file at a time
instead, because on the five inch media most units will not: /bin is
4200 blocks against a budget of 727.
.sp
The partition is printed as it is made, a line per disk with the blocks
and inodes used and the percentage of the budget, and a total.  A disk
that comes out over 100 per cent is reported rather than refused: it is
the caller's to judge, and the caller's icheck that says whether the
forecast held.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
icheck (1), mkfs (1), tar (1)
